{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>He guided his hands wittingly, for Manasseh was the firstborn.</b> Some raise the difficulty: why did Joseph keep silent until after he had blessed them, and only afterward — ״וַיִּתְמֹךְ יַד אָבִיו לְהָסִיר אוֹתָהּ מֵעַל רֹאשׁ אֶפְרַיִם״ (Genesis 48:17) — why did he not do this before he blessed them? Perhaps it is because Joseph thought that the left is more distinguished than the right, for the heart, which is the source of the intellect, is situated on the left side, whereas the liver, which is the source of the desiring soul, is on the right. And Joseph thought that since Manasseh was the firstborn, he therefore wanted to bless him with intellectual blessings — to give him knowledge and wisdom in surpassing measure — whereas he wanted to bless Ephraim with material blessings. But after he blessed them with one blessing for the two of them, Joseph then saw that this was not the path and that his father had not intended this, and perhaps he had erred in distinguishing between firstborn and ordinary, or he did not know who was standing on the right and who on the left. Therefore ״וַיֵּרַע בְּעֵינָיו וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא כֵן אָבִי״ (Genesis 48:18)." "And some raise a further difficulty: did he not see that his father guided his hands deliberately, and surely he knew what he was doing — so why was it evil in Joseph's eyes? He should have asked his father why he had guided his hands thus? And it appears that Joseph thought that Jacob assumed Joseph had taken the firstborn with his own right hand and Ephraim with his own left, so that the firstborn would then be opposite Jacob's left; therefore he guided his hands so that the firstborn would be at Jacob's right. Therefore ״וַיֵּרַע בְּעֵינָיו״ — for in truth Joseph had taken Ephraim in his right hand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:09.851682094Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}